  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b7/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_11.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Peter Parker is brooding over Betty Brant's disappearance when he hears on the radio that Doc Ock is being released from prison, having served his full sentence.&nbsp; He decides this can't be <i>allowed</i>, and Spider-Man soon swings all the way over to \"the Municipal Prison\" to register his complaint to its warden.&nbsp; But the warden tells him that Octavius has served his time and <i>must</i> be released.&nbsp; (For his part, Octavius has been behaving well while in jail in order to give him time to practice his dexterity -- he now can even light up a cigarette with just his mechanical arms!)\n\nKnowing that the warden is right, Peter nonetheless decides that he should at least keep close tabs on \"Doc Ock\" -- just in case!&nbsp; He uses his science background to devise a spider-shaped tracer, capable of sending back signals that can be picked up by a portable receiver.&nbsp; He need only attach it to Doc Ock in order to be able to track his whereabouts.\n\nReturning to the prison just as Ock is being released, he's <i>shocked</i> to see Betty Brant herself picking up Octavius from prison! Spider-Man doesn't understand what's going on, but manages to toss the new tracer onto the car, while at the same time noticing a road map flutter out one of its windows.&nbsp; Stooping to investigate, he finds that it's a map of <i>Philadelphia</i>!&nbsp; He doesn't know what to make of this clue but decides he needs to follow it through to the end.\n\nMeanwhile, in a Philadelphia prison, a man named \"Blackie\" Gaxton (a \"ruthless mobster\") is threatening his lawyer, Bennett Brant, a timid man who obviously owes him some sort of gambling debt.&nbsp; Gaxton demands to know if Brant's sister Betty is doing what he instructed.&nbsp; He will cancel the debt if Dr. Octopus springs him from jail.&nbsp; Betty gave Bennett all her money to pay his debt to Blackie and later fled to Philadelphia to prevent Peter from finding out about brother Bennett.&nbsp; She feels trapped now and wonders how Bennett expects to be free of Gaxton when he's secretly aiding in his plot to escape from prison!\n\nPeter flies to Philadelphia in order to discover the connection between Betty and Doc Ock.&nbsp; As Spider-Man, he soon finds the car, which allows him to appear as Peter Parker and surprise Betty in a neighborhood.&nbsp; Seeing that he's tracked her down, she feels foolish for running.&nbsp; She then tells him everything, that her brother is a lawyer for Blackie, the mobster.&nbsp; Peter indicates that Spider-Man is in town to keep tabs on Doc Ock, and he decides then and there that he's going to tell Betty he's Spider-Man as soon as they return to New York.\n\nMeanwhile, Doc Ock approaches the prison where Blackie is, in order to free him and collect one hundred grand for the job, which will set him up to become the \"king of crime.\"&nbsp; Spider-Man arrives too late to stop the break-out.&nbsp; But his arrival at the prison has given Blackie time to get to Bennett and Betty, kidnap them, and head off in a ship set to leave the country!&nbsp; But the car they use happens to be the one with the spider-tracer, so Spider-Man is able to track them easily.&nbsp; Bennett has paid his debt to Blackie and pleads to let both he and Betty go, but Blackie will hear none of it!\n\nWhen Spider-Man finally swings down, he turns his ankle on a coil of rope, spraining it.&nbsp; Soon he's led down to face Blackie, Ock and his goons.&nbsp; Octavius says that now he has the money, he doesn't need Blackie anymore.&nbsp; Spider-Man springs into action, pushing some of the goons into Ock, knocking him out of the room, while Blackie grabs his gun and starts shooting at Spider-Man, but when Bennett pushes Betty aside, one of the bullets hits him!&nbsp; As he lies dying, he tells Betty that it's <i>better</i> this way -- \"you can wash your hands... of all this...\"&nbsp; But Betty holds Spider-Man responsible and starts beating on his chest, while he can't believe she actually holds him <i>accountable</i>!\n\nSurprised and hurt, Spider-Man nevertheless can't stick around, as both Blackie and Ock are fleeing.&nbsp; He confronts Blackie on deck, livid with what he just did to Betty's brother, but Doc Ock arrives on the scene too, and he has to elude the grasp of those arms, all the while still hampered by the twisted ankle.&nbsp; At the same time, Blackie's goons, seeing their leader is out for the count, grab the one hundred grand for themselves and snatch Betty as well, to use her as a hostage.&nbsp; But Doc Ock takes their money and Betty, who faints.&nbsp; When Spider-Man hurls himself at him, Ock is forced to leave her behind and head off to a smaller boat, where he figures he'll be at a greater advantage since Spider-Man won't have as much room to escape his arms.\n\nBut the sudden approach of a police launch distracts the two combatants, and their small craft crashes into a piling, hurling them both into the water.&nbsp; By the time Spider-Man surfaces, Dr. Octopus has gotten away.&nbsp; The police re-arrest Blackie and rescue Betty as Spider-Man watches.&nbsp; But Ock has escaped.\n\nPeter webs up his ankle and soon joins Betty to comfort her.&nbsp; She understands now that her brother's death wasn't actually Spider-Man's <i>fault</i>, as he was just trying to help them all.&nbsp; Nevertheless, she maintains that, \"I never want to see Spider-Man again!&nbsp; I couldn't bear being reminded... of Bennett!\"\n\nWhile Peter sympathizes, he also now knows he cannot reveal his secret to her.&nbsp; He walks dejectedly away into the night...",
  "grade_one_liner": "Doctor Octopus may be on the cover, but Betty Brant is the real story this month.\nThe crime caper is serviceable enough, but the emotional fallout hits much harder.",
  "subtitle": "Betty Brant Steals Doc Ock's Comic",
  "review_text": "Once again, a mostly compelling plot -- not as engaging as last month's perhaps, as this one focused mainly on a single supporting character -- that being Betty, and we only got a very brief glimpse of Aunt May (and no others).\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=The splash page was excellent,] foreshadowing right away that, while Betty may like <i>Peter Parker</i>, this issue will result in her hating <i>Spider-Man</i>, all in front of the shadowy form of Doctor Octopus (who, of course, made his initial appearance in last April's [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3]).\n\n[ASSET: left | ItsBetty | width=11%] [ASSET: right | PhillyRoadMap | width=26%] I liked the fact that, while Doc Ock was indeed present (and was even the villain portrayed on the issue's cover), [SPAN: severity=success; text=his presence in the story was actually more tied to the plot surrounding <i>Betty</i> than it was for getting his revenge on Spider-Man.]\n[BR]\n[BR] Betty's page 4 appearance depicting her showing up at the prison in order to pick up the just-freed Octavius was probably just as surprising to <i>readers</i> as it was to the onlooking Spider-Man!\n[BR]\n[BR] We all join him in wondering:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=What is going <i>on </i>here?]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhile it's true that the previous issue hinted that there was some secret in Betty's past (and she was even approached on its page 7 by Fancy Dan, who demanded more interest money), we didn't know that the mysterious \"other\" person in her life was actually her brother, Bennett, who we meet for the first time on this issue's page 5.\n[ASSET: center | GaxtonAndBennettBrant | width=large]\nAnd now we understand what \"debt\" was (and is) being referred to.\n\nWeaving those two plot elements together like that was a [SPAN: severity=success; text=nice touch,] as it not only tied Doc Ock to the imprisoned mobster, it also established a connection between Betty <i>and</i> this same mobster through her brother.\n\nWe may well wonder how Blackie knew (all the way from Philadelphia) that Octavius was about to be released from prison in an entirely different city -- even able to hire Ock to bust him out -- but we must simply assume this was somehow made possible through his lawyer Bennett Brant.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And thus, I'll let that curiosity slide.)]\n\nBut... since I'm already scratching my head... I guess it's worth pointing out that page 7 mentions that Peter Parker actually <i>flew</i> to Philadelphia from New York City, in what <b>has</b> to be one of the shortest flights known to mankind (though one I'm sure actually <i>does</i> exist).\n\nI know we've seen no signs of the Parkers owning a car, but didn't [LINK:1963:ASM_6|ASM #6|2026-12-22] establish that Peter didn't have enough money to take a flight to Florida (to face the Lizard)?\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=Where did he now get the money to fly to Philly?]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; text=(<b><i>Sigh</i></b>... best not to <i>overthink</i> it...)]&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: I guess they didn't have Uber back then!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPeter eventually catches up to Betty, who confesses everything to him, the one she sees now has gone to the trouble of tracking her down, and we finally come to what is so obviously <b>the main thesis for this entire issue</b>, which is Peter's decision to reveal his Spider-Man identity to Betty once they return home.\n[ASSET: center | CruxOfTheStory | width=large]\n\nBut having seen the splash page, did any reader really think this would happen?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Of course not!]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; text=(<i>Seriously</i>, Stan and Steve?&nbsp; We all know better!&nbsp; You practically <i>telegraphed</i> the ending!)]\n\nDespite this, readers plowed onward, because the suspense wasn't whether Peter would confess.\n[BR] The <i>true</i> suspense was discovering [SPAN: severity=error; text=what fresh disaster] would <i>prevent</i> him from doing so!\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhat follows is more or less a mundane prison bust, motivated solely by the promise of <b>cash</b>, followed by Blackie and his goons kidnapping Betty and Bennett, all so that Spider-Man would have to swing to the rescue.\n\nThis is the one time the plot feels noticeably less inspired, especially when we learn that Otto believes this one payout will make him \"king of crime,\" and [SPAN: severity=warning; text=demonstrating how far he's devolved as a character from the brilliant scientist we met back in issue 3.]\n\nAnd yet, while the <i>character</i> might be less interesting this time around, the <i>situation</i> Stan and Steve put him in is <b>not</b>...\n\n[ASSET: left | GreatPanel | width=15%] When Spider-Man finally shows up, and all the major players occupy the same room (on page 11), I have to say that Steve Ditko follows this up with [SPAN: severity=success; text=a fantastic \"menacing\" pose for Dr. Octopus.]\n[BR]\n[BR] That first panel on the next page is the best, with the view aimed up at Ock's face from just below, at the level of his arms.&nbsp; I recall this is the <i>second</i> time Steve's pulled this off with such skill, as I remember issue #3 had a great look as well.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Future artist Gil Kane is <i>also</i> known for his low panels aimed upward at characters' heads, which gives them a unique look, and this one reminds me very much of that style.]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Well done!&nbsp; Love that panel!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe speed at which the narration goes from mayhem, to Bennett's shooting death, to Betty's \"<b>I hate you!</b>\" directed at Spider-Man may have been a little much, but it is nevertheless poignant, and Ditko does a great job showing Spider-Man's obvious hurt even without being able to feature his facial expressions through the mask.\n[ASSET: center | BettyHatesSpiderMan | width=large]\n\nNaturally, Spider-Man has no choice but to leave Betty behind and chase after Blackie and Doctor Octopus, both of whom are escaping, and the ensuing conflict consumes most of the rest of the story.\n\n[ASSET: right | CantTellHerNow | width=17%] Doc Ock gets away, Blackie is re-captured, and Spider-Man switches back to Peter Parker to rush to Betty's side, where he learns that, even though she finally understands Bennett's death wasn't Spider-Man's fault, she still never wants to see him again; it would only remind her of what she has lost.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=And there you have it:]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=<i>another planned Parker moment goes awry!</i>]\n\n[ASSET: left | FinalPanel | width=16%] The issue's final panel of Peter walking away into the night is <i>very</i> reminiscent of the final panel of <i>Amazing Fantasy</i> 15 ([LINK:1962:AMAZFANT_15_asm0|AMAZFANT #15]), except here, Ditko adds the huge, looming figure of Spider-Man to the mix, hovering over Peter in a hauntingly eerie pose.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: One that longtime readers have grown all too familiar with in the pages of the Spider-Man mags in the decades since.]\n[BR]\n[BR] I can't recall for sure, but this may be the first time the pose is captured in the artwork, and [SPAN: severity=info; text=it's very effective to show how the specter of Spider-Man hovers over the events of Peter's life.]\n\nMeanwhile... Doc Ock's escape at the end leaves us with another dangling thread that brings us again back to the table for another issue next month!\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=As I've indicated before, the writing on this magazine so far has been hands-down better than any of the other offerings.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe story wasn't as all-encompassing as last time and involved far fewer members of Spider-Man's supporting cast, but that didn't matter:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=it involved the <i>correct</i> ones.]\n\nAlong the way, the release of Doctor Octopus \"accidentally\" led Spider-Man to discover what had happened to Betty last month.&nbsp; And I use the term \"accidentally\" with tongue firmly planted in cheek, as it was clear this was just one more tenet of the [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Comic Book Convenience 101] Course Guide [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(copyright pending)] played out exactly as scripted.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=It didn't matter.]\n\nIn the end, the crux of the issue wasn't the cover villain at all (though I know Marvel -- and thus editor Stan Lee -- had to showcase him in order to plug the magazine).&nbsp; The <i>true</i> story this month was what was on the title's splash page, namely, Betty pounding on the chest of Spider-Man in pure, unadulterated hatred (with just the mere <i>shadow</i> of Doc Ock cast on the wall).\n\n<i>True</i>, Ock was peripherally involved, and there was no <i>shortage</i> of largely lackluster fisticuffs, but readers <i>knew</i>... [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=that was only for show] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(and indeed, Ock gets away)].\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=The real story was always about the mystery of Betty's past], and the fact that Spider-Man now serves as a reminder of what Betty lost... <i>well</i>... that's just more collateral damage that always seems to follow in the wake of both Peter Parker and Spider-Man, even though his magazine is still only 11 issues old.\n\n[HANG0] That reminiscent final panel serves as a gut-wrenching reminder of this:\n[BR] -- that the specter of Spider-Man follows Peter... <i>everywhere</i>.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It demonstrates yet again how Spider-Man is grounded in tragedy far more than any other character.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * This issue features the first appearance of the Spider-Tracer, and in at least this iteration, Peter is forced to use a mechanical device in order to receive its signal.&nbsp; It's yet another instance of \"science buff\" Peter Parker's equipping himself by adding new gadgets to his arsenal.\n\n[HANG0] * The Spider Signal is once again featured at the top of page 3, where Spider-Man reveals himself to the warden (which writer Stan Lee describes as a \"calling card\" in the narration).\n\n[HANG0] * This is also the first time that Doctor Octopus is referred to as the shortened \"Doc Ock\" (see bottom of page 3)."
  ],
  "letters_page": "I loved Joseph Shea's letter -- he obviously <i>hates</i> the notion of the two-part story and says \"If I wanted a continuous story, I would read the comics in the paper -- for free!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(<i>Hmm</i>, does this kid know that somebody <i>pays</i> for his morning paper every day?)]\n\nChristine Cassello's letter questions Peter being with Betty when she asks \"how old is Jameson's secretary?\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Heh</i>, which is a point I've made myself!)]",
  "highlight": "Doc Ock returns, Betty's secret is revealed, and Peter's latest chance at happiness falls apart."
}